            {
              "id": "q1_2015_g2",
              "num": "1(b)",
              "text": "Discuss the 73rd and 74th Amendments to the Indian Constitution as milestones in democratic decentralization.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "Answer Outline:\n1. **Context**: Constitutionalized local self-governments in 1992, appending Parts IX and IX-A and 11th/12th Schedules.\n2. **Milestones**: Formalized mandatory 5-year elections supervised by SECs, set up State Finance Commissions for funding allocations, and secured 33% mandatory reservation for women.\n3. **Friction Points**: Devolution of the 'three Fs' (Funds, Functions, and Functionaries) is often bypassed by reluctant state administrations.\n4. **Conclusion**: Bridging decentralization gaps requires building administrative and financial capacity at the grass-roots level."
            },
            {
              "id": "q1_2015_g3",
              "num": "1(c)",
              "text": "Explain the features of the Swadeshi Movement (1905-1908) as a defining phase of Indian nationalism.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "Answer Outline:\n1. **Origin**: Sparked directly by Lord Curzon’s unilateral partition of Bengal in 1905.\n2. **Key Features**: Boycotting foreign-manufactured cloth with ceremonial bonfires, established national education committees, and promoted indigenous enterprises (e.g., Bengal Chemical Stores).\n3. **Significance**: Successfully integrated masses (women, laborers, students) beyond upper-class elites, introducing Swaraj as the primary political goal.\n4. **Conclusion**: Provided the active boycott and passive resistance strategic foundation for Gandhi's future national campaigns."
            },
            {
              "id": "q1_2015_g4",
              "num": "1(d)",
              "text": "Contrast the Fundamental Rights (Part III) with the Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV) in the Indian Constitution.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "Answer Outline:\n1. **Nature**: Fundamental Rights (FRs) are negative obligations on the state, individualistic, and legally enforceable. Directive Principles of State Policy (DPSPs) are positive commands, socialistic, and non-justiciable.\n2. **Objective**: FRs establish political democracy and individual liberties. DPSPs strive to establish social and economic democracy, crafting a Welfare State.\n3. **Friction & Harmony**: Historically contested (e.g., Minerva Mills, 1980), courts have concluded they are complementary, forming the dynamic 'moral conscience' of the Constitution.\n4. **Conclusion**: True welfare requires balanced progress implementing DPSPs without compromising core FRs."
            },
            {
              "id": "q1_2015_g5",
              "num": "1(e)",
              "text": "Analyze the significance of the Basic Structure Doctrine established in the Kesavananda Bharati case (1973).",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "Answer Outline:\n1. **Context**: Established in 1973 by a historic 13-judge bench, constraining Parliament's amending powers under Article 368.\n2. **Core Principle**: While Parliament is authorized to modify any constitutional part, it is strictly forbidden from destroying its fundamental identity or 'Basic Structure'.\n3. **Illustrative Pillars**: Secularism, federalism, judicial review, supremacy of the Constitution, and sovereign-democratic-republic nature.\n4. **Significance**: Shielded fundamental liberties from temporary majoritarian overreaches, maintaining constitutional supremacy and institutional checks and balances."
            }
