              "id": "q3_d",
              "num": "3(d)",
              "text": "A rising income and wealth inequality in India may not be good for India's long-term goals. Do you agree? Critically discuss.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "Yes, rising wealth and income inequality (as reported by Oxfam) poses severe risks to India's long-term development:\nCritical impacts include:\n1. Stifled Domestic Demand: Concentration of wealth in few hands reduces the purchasing power of the lower and middle classes, dampening aggregate consumption-driven growth.\n2. Human Capital Deficit: Poor families struggle to afford quality healthcare and education, reducing overall labor force productivity.\n3. Social Cohesion: Extreme disparities fuel social unrest, crime, and class divides, undermining political stability and investor confidence.\n4. Sustainable Development: Directly clashes with SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities).\nTherefore, while wealth creation is necessary, inclusive growth through progressive taxation, robust social welfare programs, and job creation is vital for India's long-term prosperity."
