              "suggestedAnswer": "Magadha's rise to dominance among the Mahajanapadas was driven by several key factors:\n1. **Strategic Geography:** Capital cities (Rajgir, surrounded by hills, and Pataliputra, at the confluence of major rivers) offered natural military defense.\n2. **Resource Richness:** Proximity to the iron ore deposits of the Chotanagpur plateau enabled the production of superior weapons and agricultural implements.\n3. **Agricultural Surplus:** Located in the fertile middle-Gangetic plains, generating substantial tax revenues.\n4. **Military Innovations:** First to systematically deploy elephants in warfare.\n5. **Ambitious Rulers:** Leadership of capable and aggressive monarchs like Bimbisara, Ajatashatru, and Mahapadma Nanda who pursued expansionist policies."
