          "questions": [
            {
              "id": "q4_a",
              "num": "4(a)",
              "text": "\"Prime Minister is the nucleus of the Indian Political System.\" Discuss.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "The Prime Minister of India acts as the real executive authority and the 'nucleus' of the political architecture because:\n1. **Head of Government:** The PM exercises supreme administrative power, recommends the appointment/dismissal of ministers, and allocates portfolios.\n2. **Leader of Parliament:** Acts as the leader of the majority party in the Lok Sabha, steering the legislative agenda and national policy.\n3. **Link between President and Cabinet:** Under Article 78, the PM is the primary channel of communication between the President and the Council of Ministers.\n4. **Centralization of Authority:** The growth of the Prime Minister's Office (PMO) has concentrated key strategic decisions, foreign policy, and emergency planning under direct PM oversight, reflecting a presidential style of parliamentary governance."
            },
            {
              "id": "q4_b",
              "num": "4(b)",
              "text": "Critically examine the idea of \"One Nation, One Election\" in India.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "The concept of simultaneous elections for the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies has several pros and cons:\n**Arguments in Favor:**\n1. **Cost Efficiency:** Reduces the immense and recurring expenditure of holding separate elections.\n2. **Governance Continuity:** Prevents administrative paralysis caused by the frequent enforcement of the Model Code of Conduct (MCC).\n3. **Security Mobilization:** Reduces the frequent deployment of security forces for election duties.\n\n**Arguments Against:**\n1. **Federalism Concerns:** National issues may overshadow regional concerns, disadvantaging regional parties.\n2. **Constitutional Hurdles:** Requires complex constitutional amendments (regarding the terms of Lok Sabha/Assemblies, mid-term dissolutions, etc.).\n3. **Logistical Challenges:** Requires a massive scale-up of EVMs, VVPATs, and polling personnel."
            },
            {
              "id": "q4_c",
              "num": "4(c)",
              "text": "Explain the discretionary powers of the President of India.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "While the President of India is a constitutional head bound by the advice of the Council of Ministers (Article 74), they possess key situational and constitutional discretionary powers:\n1. **Appointment of Prime Minister:** When no single party or pre-poll alliance secures a clear majority in the Lok Sabha, the President exercises discretion to select a PM candidate.\n2. **Dismissal of Council of Ministers:** The President can dismiss the ministry if it loses the confidence of the Lok Sabha and refuses to resign.\n3. **Dissolution of Lok Sabha:** Can dissolve the house if the Council of Ministers loses its majority and no alternative government can be formed.\n4. **Suspensive and Pocket Veto:** Can return non-money bills for reconsideration or sit on a bill indefinitely (pocket veto) as seen in the Indian Post Office (Amendment) Bill, 1986."
            },
            {
              "id": "q4_d",
              "num": "4(d)",
              "text": "Describe the constitutional provisions regarding the Finance Commission of India and explain its functions.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "The Finance Commission (FC) is a quasi-judicial body constituted under **Article 280** of the Constitution. It is appointed by the President of India every five years (or earlier).\n**Core Functions:**\n1. **Distribution of Tax Proceeds:** Recommends the distribution of net proceeds of taxes between the Union and the States (vertical devolution) and the allocation among states (horizontal devolution).\n2. **Grants-in-Aid:** Lays down the principles governing grants-in-aid to the states out of the Consolidated Fund of India (Article 275).\n3. **Augmenting State Funds:** Suggests measures to augment the Consolidated Fund of a State to supplement the resources of Panchayats and Municipalities.\n4. **Presidential Referrals:** Addresses any other matter referred to it by the President in the interest of sound public finance."
            },
            {
              "id": "q4_e",
              "num": "4(e)",
              "text": "Give justification for India's claim for permanent membership of the United Nations Security Council.",
              "marks": 5,
              "suggestedAnswer": "India's claim for a permanent seat on the UNSC is supported by several factors:\n1. **Demographics & Democracy:** India is the world's most populous nation and the largest thriving democracy, representing more than one-sixth of humanity.\n2. **Economic Influence:** India is the fifth-largest global economy and a key driver of international trade and technology.\n3. **Peacekeeping Contributions:** Historically one of the largest troop-contributing nations to UN Peacekeeping operations, having deployed over 2.7 lakh personnel across various missions.\n4. **Responsible Global Actor:** Possession of nuclear capabilities with a declared 'no first use' policy, and an active role in climate action, disaster relief (e.g., Vaccine Maitri), and promoting rules-based international order."
            }
          ]
