              "suggestedAnswer": "TUTOR THEME SUMMARY & VOCABULARY KEY:\n- Core Theme: The passage outlines how environmental degradation escalated from localized, manageable incidents into an urgent global crisis by the 21st century. It traces international responses beginning with utilitarian agreements (river management and fishing quotas) to ethical preservation efforts (bird conventions, wildlife parks), culminating in the modern environmental movement of the 1960s sparked by scientific alarms (e.g., DDT usage, radioactive fallout, and marine oil spills).\n- Key Vocabulary: 'Unabated' (without losing intensity), 'Ubiquitous' (found everywhere), 'Over-exploitation' (excessive use), 'Landmark treaty' (pivotal agreement), 'Transboundary pollution' (pollution crossing national borders).\n\nMODEL PRÉCIS:\nBy the early twenty-first century, environmental degradation became a prominent global concern. While environmental impact was historically localized, widespread industrialization and population growth transformed these issues into a planetary crisis marked by overfishing, deforestation, massive waste dumping, and climate change.\n\nHistorically, international environmental politics began in the nineteenth century, initially to regulate economic assets like shared rivers. Early treaties, such as those establishing the IMO in 1948 and marine pollution guidelines in 1954, primarily protected industrial interests. Similarly, initial biodiversity agreements targeted agricultural pests, protected commercial species like fur seals, or regulated whaling. However, public pressure gradually fostered an ethical concern for wildlife preservation, leading to the establishment of early game reserves.\n\nThe 1960s marked a dramatic shift. Rachel Carson's book 'Silent Spring' raised critical awareness of pesticide hazards, catalyzing the modern environmental movement. Concurrently, public anxiety over atmospheric nuclear tests, high-profile oil tanker disasters, transboundary acid rain, and debates over the Law of the Sea underscored the globalized nature of environmental threats, forcing nations to develop collaborative regulatory frameworks. (187 words)"
